.TH FLOWDOCKS 1 "September 2026" "FlowDocks 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
flowdocks \- application dock for X11 desktops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flowdocks
.RB [ \-\-toggle " | " \-\-preferences ]
.RB [ \-\-smoke-test ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
FlowDocks provides application shortcuts, running-window indicators and
configurable screen-edge placement. X11 with a compositing window manager
is the supported environment. Native Wayland integration is limited.
Starting it again reveals the existing dock on the same display.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-toggle
Send a local IPC request to show or hide the running dock.
Start the dock if it is not running.
.TP
.B \-\-preferences
Send a local IPC request to open preferences in the running dock,
or start the dock with preferences open.
.TP
.B \-\-smoke-test
Render briefly and exit without saving settings. Bypasses single-instance
IPC and does not register the global shortcut or show a tray icon.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command-line help and exit.
.SH CONTROLS
Click an application to launch it or focus its running window.
Shift-click to launch another instance. Right-click for the context menu.
.PP
Open the application picker with the diamond icon. Drag an application
from the picker onto the dock to pin it. Drag dock icons to reorder them;
drop a pinned icon outside the dock to unpin it.
.PP
A launcher dropped from the desktop, a file manager or the applications
menu is matched to the installed application by path, desktop-file ID and
Exec line, so a copy or symlink of an installed entry pins correctly. A
launcher that is not installed is refused.
.PP
Drag the clock, grip or empty dock surface to move the dock. In the
default edge positioning it snaps to the nearest screen edge; in free
positioning it stays wherever it is dropped, including flush against an
edge. A free dock can also be rotated between a horizontal bar and a
vertical column; a docked one follows the edge it is attached to. Enable "Lock dock position"
in preferences to prevent this movement; it does not lock icon editing.
Free positioning has no screen edge, so auto-hide and the edge hotspot
are disabled there and the dock is hidden with the shortcut, tray or menu.
.PP
On X11, the default global shortcut is Ctrl+Alt+A to toggle visibility.
Enable, disable or change it in preferences. If another application owns
the shortcut, choose another combination or bind
.B flowdocks \-\-toggle
in the desktop keyboard settings.
.SH PREFERENCES
Use the sliders icon or
.BR "flowdocks \-\-preferences" .
Screen layer choices are normal, above other windows, and below other
windows. Revealing temporarily raises the dock until the pointer leaves;
the window manager controls final stacking behavior.
.PP
Edge action choices are reveal, toggle and off. Hover briefly at the
dock's screen edge to trigger the configured action, then leave the edge
to rearm it. Auto-hide is configured separately. Preferences also include
screen and edge selection, appearance, the global shortcut, position lock
and optional user-controlled login autostart.
.SH DESKTOP PANEL
The context menu and preferences can hide, show, move and open the
settings of the desktop's own panel. XFCE panels are changed through
.BR xfconf-query ,
MATE and Cinnamon panels through their GSettings keys. LXQt, KDE, GNOME
and Budgie offer only their panel settings dialog, because their panels
cannot be hidden or moved reliably from the command line. Actions a
desktop does not support are shown greyed out with the reason. FlowDocks
changes only the current user's panel configuration and never runs as root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/flowdocks/settings.json
User preferences; defaults to
.I ~/.config/flowdocks/settings.json
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/flowdocks/README.md
Additional usage and desktop requirements.
